			<div class="content" id="post-content-4844230">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Horror</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Standard SCP format with the containment procedures, description, incident log and a log of several SCP-XXXX instances (Possibly collaborative).</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> Incorporeality is a a common power of anomalies. In most cases this is accompanied by some form of levitation. But happened to all those people who have accomplished the former, but not the latter?</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The SCP is a group of incorporeal objects located at the center of the earth, the majority of which are human corpses. All of these corpses are located at a single point, the gravitational center of the earth, with there being a staggering amount of bodies, over a billion. It is in fact the resting place of every person who has became incorporeal and fallen through the ground. The way that the foundation learned about this anomaly is that a group of foundation researchers and soldiers got trapped in the mass after being turn incorporeal by the CI during an attack. Transmissions received from this group allowed the foundation to locate and document it and their communication with them emphasizes how horrible it is in there. Analysis reveals many of the people in the core are still alive and unable to change their situation. Given that the agents are all essentially blind, deaf and mute due to them being entirely encased in solid matter, they communicate with the foundation through an incorporeal computer that fell with them, which utilizes anomalous communication technology. On behalf of the foundation they attempt to document their experience (as they are unaware of the nature of their situation) and eventually figure out a way escape from it, but can't. They lose contact with the foundation when it is revealed that their form of intangibility used on them wares off, leaving them to be crushed by the intense pressure. The last communication is a call for help, begging for rescue, not only for them but for any other people trapped like they are. A note at the end describes how the foundation isn't pursuing this avenue of research due to logistics and a lack of benefits.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Taking a semi-used joke to its logical conclusion.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> In this article 'intangibility' also extends to 'partial immunity from outside stimuli' explaining why many of the people in the core are still alive. The ones that are dead are the ones who's intangibility wore off slowly enough that they starved, dehydrated, overheated or died form the pressure. Others died of old age.</p>

