<p>I’m not going to delve too deeply into the scientific issues here, since that’s very much not my department; however, I will mention that this assumes incorporeal entities would be able to touch each other, which is an interesting choice. It also seems as though the heat from the earth’s core would make it especially difficult for anything to remain sentient, unless incorporeality also affects temperature.</p>
<p>Moving on, I’m afraid I still can’t see much of a narrative here, despite your response to <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/grigori-karpin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4558017); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4558017&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608713829" alt="Grigori Karpin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4558017)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/grigori-karpin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4558017); return false;">Grigori Karpin</a></span>. The existence of a group of Foundation personnel within the core isn’t a narrative so much as a set-up. What do you plan to do with them?</p>
<p>Finally, if units are an issue for you, I’d recommend this article on <a href="https://what-if.xkcd.com/4/" target="_blank">a similar scenario</a>.</p>
