			<div class="content" id="post-content-4853679">
									

<p>Thanks for the feedback. I will try and clarify a few things:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Clarifications</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Why is this happening?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because over the years many people have became intangible and fallen to the center of the earth.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why is the majority of objects at the center “human corpses”?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because humans are the most common thing to turn intangible and fall through the fall due to our natural desire for power, in this case, the power to walk through walls. Naturally there are also other animals and inanimate objects in there as well.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How is it possible that they number in the billions?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This anomaly has been building up over many thousands of years. The bodies come from as far-flung sources as ancient trickster gods, experimental para-technology from an Occult war and quite a few from previous iterations of <a href="/scp-2000">SCP-2000</a>. Lastly while most bodies are dead, none have decomposed due to their intangible nature.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why are the agents “blind, deaf and mute”? If so, how do they even use a computer?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The agents are &quot;blind, deaf and mute&quot; due to how their eyes ears and mouths are located entirely within the solid matter of the earth inner core. They use a computer because it was in contact with one of them when they became intangible, staying intangible as well and allowing the intangible agent to use it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Similarly, why does the effect suddenly wear off and they die?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because all types of intangibly eventually wear off after a certain amount of time within a solid or liquid. They agents die when they become tangible enough to suffocate.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Lastly, the Foundation not pursuing containment of the anomaly is not in line with their motto.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The Foundation isn't containing it because:<br />
1. It's effectively self-containing due to it's location.<br />
2. There is not feasible way to remove it from it's location.<br />
3. The threat to exposure to the public is extremely small.<br />
4. If they were somehow able to remove it, it would inevitably end up there again upon the first containment breach.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Basically, the MTF goes incorporeal, communicate with the Foundation, and then they die? You need more than that, especially if you want to give your readers some semblance of answers as to what’s going on.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The narrative of the article is the story of that MTF and their gradual transformation from hopeful, to weary to despairing and begging for rescue. At first they help the foundation in any way they can by communicating with them an informing them of the anomaly and trying to help them document it. After a while they realize they won't be rescued. As for answers, there are none. This isn't a sinister plot or experiment gone wrong, this is an inevitability in a world with intangibility.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Other suggestions might be to explain the origins of the anomaly, how/why the CI is involved and how exactly the Foundation goes about trying to contain this.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>1. The anomaly is the collection of everyone who has ever become intangible and fallen through the floor.<br />
2. The CI is a plot device explaining how an entire MTF was turned intangible. They have little importance to the anomaly overall. The CI could be change to the SH or an experiment gone wrong.<br />
3. The Foundation can't/won't/doesn't need to contain the anomaly due to it's nature, being content with observation and documentation.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Hope this clears up few things up!</p>

							</div>

								
